// NE5532 Hi-Fi Headphone Preamp - Multimeter Test Procedure
//
// This is a pure-analog project. There is no microcontroller in the
// signal path. Use the steps below with a DMM (digital multimeter) to
// confirm the circuit is alive before you plug in headphones.
//
// What you need:
//   - The completed breadboard, NOT YET connected to an audio source
//   - A fresh 9V battery (or 9V wall-wart)
//   - A DMM set to DC volts
//
// SAFETY: keep headphones UNPLUGGED for steps 1-4. Op-amps occasionally
// oscillate on first power-up and a loud click into your ears is no fun.
//
// Test-point map (also marked on the wiring diagram):
//   TP1 = +V rail (NE5532 pin 8)
//   TP2 = Virtual ground node (junction of the two 22K resistors,
//         the 100uF 16V cap, and the far end of both 10K input bias
//         resistors)
//   TP3 = Left channel output, AFTER the 1uF coupling cap
//   TP4 = Right channel output, AFTER the 1uF coupling cap
//   GND = NE5532 pin 4 / battery minus
//
// ----------------------------------------------------------------------
// Step 1 - Power rail check (no input signal)
// ----------------------------------------------------------------------
// Probe between TP1 (NE5532 pin 8, V+) and GND (pin 4).
// Expected: 8.5 V to 9.2 V DC on a fresh battery.
// If you see 0 V: battery dead, polarity reversed, or rail wire missing.
// If you see less than 6.5 V: the NE5532 needs at least ~6 V across
// pins 8 and 4 to behave. Below that it clips and distorts. A tired
// 9V alkaline under load can sag here - swap in a fresh battery before
// blaming the circuit.
//
// ----------------------------------------------------------------------
// Step 2 - Virtual ground check
// ----------------------------------------------------------------------
// Probe between TP2 (virtual ground) and GND. TP2 is the breadboard
// row where BOTH 22K resistors meet the 100uF 16V cap. It is also the
// "far end" of both 10K input bias resistors - so an easy visual cue
// is to follow either 10K back from NE5532 pin 3 or pin 5 until both
// 10Ks land in the same row.
// Expected: approximately HALF the supply voltage, around 4.3 V to 4.6 V.
// If you see 0 V or 9 V: one of the 22K bias resistors is missing or
// shorted. If it slowly drifts: the 100uF 16V cap is in backwards.
//
// ----------------------------------------------------------------------
// Step 3 - Output DC offset (the most important measurement)
// ----------------------------------------------------------------------
// Probe TP3 (after the LEFT 1uF output coupling cap, where the wire
// leaves toward the headphone jack tip) with the other lead on GND.
// Then repeat for TP4 (Right channel, ring side).
// Expected: between -50 mV and +50 mV DC on each. Ideally under 10 mV.
// If you see several volts: the output cap is shorted, or it is in
// backwards (+ leg should face the op-amp output pin).
// Headphone voice coils HATE DC. Do not plug headphones in until both
// channels read under 100 mV.
//
// ----------------------------------------------------------------------
// Step 4 - Op-amp pin sanity check
// ----------------------------------------------------------------------
// With no input signal, all of these pins should sit near the virtual
// ground voltage you measured in Step 2 (within +/- 50 mV):
//   Pin 1 (U1A output), Pin 2 (U1A inverting in), Pin 3 (U1A non-inv in)
//   Pin 5 (U1B non-inv in), Pin 6 (U1B inverting in), Pin 7 (U1B output)
// If any pin sits at 0 V or 9 V (the rails), that channel is latched up:
// usually a missing feedback wire or input bias resistor.
//
// ----------------------------------------------------------------------
// Step 5 - Listening test
// ----------------------------------------------------------------------
// Power off. Connect a phone or laptop headphone output to the input
// jack. Plug your headphones into the output jack. Turn the source
// volume DOWN to about 20%. Power on.
// Expected: clean, slightly louder, low-noise audio. Slowly raise the
// source volume. There should be no hiss with the input unplugged and
// no audible hum.
// If you hear a low buzz: the virtual-ground 100uF cap may be too small
// or missing. If you hear distortion at high volume: the source is
// clipping the input (this preamp has unity gain - it cannot magically
// add headroom). If distortion appears at any volume and TP1 reads
// under ~6.5 V, the battery is sagging - replace it.

