// Finger-Freezer Cold Spot - NO MICROCONTROLLER REQUIRED
//
// This is a "wow" build with zero code. The "program" you follow
// is a multimeter probe procedure to PROVE the cold plate is doing
// what physics says it should do.
//
// (Use any digital multimeter you already own - it is a tool, not
// a stocked part of this build.)
//
// ============================================================
// MULTIMETER PROBE PROCEDURE
// ============================================================
//
// SAFETY FIRST
//   1. Heatsink is glued to the HOT side of the Peltier with
//      thermal paste BEFORE you plug anything in. No heatsink =
//      Peltier dies in about 30 seconds and gets dangerously hot.
//   2. Cold side faces UP. That is the finger side.
//   3. Run the Peltier at 5V from the buck converter, NOT 12V.
//      5V keeps the cold side gently cool (around 5-10 C below
//      room) so a 10-year-old can touch it safely. 12V drives it
//      to freezing and frost and is too aggressive for a first build.
//   4. MULTIMETER 10A SOCKET WARNING: when you measure current in
//      Step 4 you move the RED probe into the meter's 10A socket.
//      After that measurement, move the probe BACK to the V/Ohm
//      socket BEFORE switching the dial to Volts. A meter set to
//      Volts with the probe still in the 10A socket becomes a dead
//      short across whatever you touch and will blow the meter's
//      10A fuse (or worse).
//
// ============================================================
// HIGH-CURRENT PATH (important)
// ============================================================
//   The Peltier pulls 1-2 A. That is at the upper edge of what a
//   small 400-tie-point breadboard and DuPont contacts handle
//   cleanly - rails warm up, contacts get resistive, voltage sags.
//
//   So: run the Peltier's RED and BLACK leads on DIRECT DuPont
//   jumpers from the buck converter output (or MB102 screw
//   terminals if you wired them there), NOT through the
//   breadboard tie-points. The breadboard rails are used ONLY as
//   neat clip-on points for the multimeter probes.
//
// ============================================================
// STEP 1 - Set the buck converter output
// ============================================================
//   - Plug the 12V wall-wart into the buck converter input.
//   - Many "6-24V to 5V 3A USB Buck Converter" boards are fixed at
//     5V. If yours has a trim pot, turn the multimeter to DC Volts
//     (20V range), put black probe on the USB output GND, red probe
//     on the USB output +5V, and adjust until the meter reads
//     between 4.9 and 5.1 V.
//   - WRITE DOWN the reading. This is "Vsupply".
//
// ============================================================
// STEP 2 - Hook the MB102 onto the breadboard (rails only)
// ============================================================
//   - Push the MB102 power module onto the 400-point breadboard so
//     its pins bridge both power rails.
//   - Set BOTH jumpers on the MB102 to 5V.
//   - Feed the MB102 from the buck converter USB output (USB-A to
//     barrel-jack cable, OR jumper wires from the buck output to
//     the MB102 VIN / GND pins).
//   - Press the MB102 power button. The LED on the MB102 lights.
//   - NOTE: 5V in -> 5V out means the MB102's regulator is just
//     passing the rail through. We are using it purely as a
//     rail-distribution board so the multimeter has clean probe
//     points. The buck converter is doing the real regulating.
//   - Multimeter check (probe in V/Ohm socket): red probe on red
//     rail, black probe on blue rail. Should read about 5 V.
//
// ============================================================
// STEP 3 - Connect the Peltier (DIRECT, not via the rails)
// ============================================================
//   - The Peltier has two wires, RED and BLACK.
//   - Run DuPont jumpers DIRECTLY from the buck converter output
//     (or MB102 screw terminals) to the Peltier leads:
//       Buck +5V  ->  Peltier RED
//       Buck GND  ->  Peltier BLACK
//   - Do NOT push the Peltier leads through the breadboard tie
//     points for the long-term run. The breadboard rails are only
//     there for the meter to clip onto.
//   - If you wire it backwards, the cold and hot sides SWAP. The
//     finger side will get hot instead of cold. This is a great
//     thing to demonstrate.
//
// ============================================================
// STEP 4 - Measure what is happening
// ============================================================
//   - Within 30 seconds, touch the cold side (top) with the back
//     of your finger. It should feel noticeably cool.
//   - Touch the heatsink (after about 1 minute). It should feel
//     warm. The Peltier is MOVING heat from one face to the other.
//   - VOLTS check: with the red probe still in the V/Ohm socket,
//     dial to DC Volts, measure across the Peltier leads -
//     should still read about 5 V.
//   - CURRENT check (do this LAST):
//       a. Power off.
//       b. Move the RED probe from V/Ohm to the 10A socket.
//       c. Turn the dial to DC current (10A range).
//       d. Break the +5V wire to the Peltier RED lead and put the
//          meter in-line (red probe from buck +5V, black probe to
//          Peltier RED).
//       e. Power on. It should read between 1.0 and 2.0 A. WRITE
//          THIS DOWN. This is "Ipeltier".
//       f. Power off, restore the direct wire, and IMMEDIATELY
//          move the red probe back to the V/Ohm socket.
//          Never leave a probe in the 10A socket while switching
//          back to Volts.
//   - Power used = Vsupply x Ipeltier. Probably about 5 W to 10 W
//     of heat being pumped.
//
// ============================================================
// STEP 5 - The swap experiment
// ============================================================
//   - Unplug the 12V wall-wart.
//   - Swap the Peltier RED and BLACK wires at the buck output.
//   - Plug back in. Now the FORMERLY cold side is hot, and the
//     heatsink side (which you cannot easily touch) is cold.
//   - Swap them back. Done.
//
// ============================================================
// WHAT THE NUMBERS MEAN
// ============================================================
//   Vsupply  ~ 5.0 V   (gentle, kid-safe)
//   Ipeltier ~ 1-2 A   (Peltier is a hungry device even at 5V)
//   Power    ~ 5-10 W  (this is why the heatsink MUST be there)
//
// No setup() / loop() needed - the Peltier IS the program.

